820 0 obj
<<
/Title (To move fast, we kept our data preparation extremely simple:)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 789 0 R
/Prev 811 0 R
/Next 823 0 R
>>
endobj
821 0 obj
<<
/Title (We turned on GPU acceleration \(using a graphics card instead of a standard processor\) so the model could learn from millions of rows in just a few seconds.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 789 0 R
/Prev 824 0 R
/Next 812 0 R
>>
endobj
822 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Code Changes: n_estimators \(Number of Trees\): Increased from 100 to 200. This gives the model 100 extra iterations to learn from its mistakes and fine-tune its predictions based on the new time features.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 743 0 R
/Prev 813 0 R
/Next 814 0 R
>>
endobj
823 0 obj
<<
/Title (Kept it to the Numbers: We only fed the model raw numerical data \(like temperatures and angles\). We temporarily threw out complex text data, like the Martian timestamps \(LMST and LTST\), to save time.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 789 0 R
/Prev 820 0 R
/Next 824 0 R
>>
endobj
824 0 obj
<<
/Title (Martian sensor data is messy and full of blank spots \(NaNs\). Instead of trying to manually guess and fill in those blanks, we used an algorithm called XGBoost. XGBoost is smart enough to handle missing data automatically without breaking.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 789 0 R
/Prev 823 0 R
/Next 821 0 R
>>
endobj
